 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Camera Intrinsic Matrix Construction](3112-camera-intrinsic-matrix-construction.md)
+Constructs the 3x3 camera intrinsic matrix $K$ from focal length and image dimensions, serving as the bridge between pixel coordinates and normalized device coordinates (NDC) for ray generation. It differs from [Pinhole Camera Ray Generation](5058-pinhole-camera-ray-generation.md) by explicitly building the calibration matrix $K$ rather than assuming a canonical pinhole model with unit focal length and center at the image center. Choose it when precise calibration parameters are available.
+
+### [Pinhole Camera Ray Generation](5058-pinhole-camera-ray-generation.md)
+Computes the origin and direction vectors for rays corresponding to each pixel on an image plane, assuming an ideal pinhole camera model with no lens distortion or depth of field. It explicitly constructs the view frustum geometry (basis vectors and view plane bounds) from high-level camera parameters (eye position, look-at point, up vector, and field of view) rather than relying on a pre-computed projection matrix. Choose it for simple, parameter-driven camera setups without explicit calibration matrices.
+
 ### Camera Intrinsics Simplification
-Constructs the 3x3 camera intrinsic matrix $K$ assuming zero skew and aspect ratio of 1 [S31, unofficial]. The matrix is defined as $K = \begin{bmatrix} f_{\text{camera}} & 0 & W/2 \\ 0 & f_{\text{camera}} & H/2 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}$, where $f_{\text{camera}}$ is the focal length, $W$ is image width, and $H$ is image height [S31, unofficial]. This simplification assumes square pixels and a principal point at the image center [S31, unofficial].
+Constructs the 3x3 camera intrinsic matrix $K$ assuming zero skew and an aspect ratio of 1 [S31, unofficial]. The matrix is defined as $K = \begin{bmatrix} f_{\text{camera}} & 0 & W/2 \\ 0 & f_{\text{camera}} & H/2 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}$, where $f_{\text{camera}}$ is the focal length, $W$ is image width, and $H$ is image height [S31, unofficial]. This simplification assumes square pixels and a principal point at the image center [S31, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$ to generate rays for all pixels [G]. The operations are element-wise vector math, highly parallelizable on GPU.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$ to store the origin and direction tensors.
-*   **Strengths:** Explicit control over camera calibration; supports arbitrary principal points and focal lengths; standard in NeRF and modern rendering pipelines.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires accurate calibration data; sensitive to errors in $K$ or $T_{c2w}$; division by zero if focal length is 0.
-*   **When to use:** When camera parameters are known from calibration (e.g., COLMAP, sensor data) or when precise control over projection geometry is needed.
-*   **When not to use:** For simple orthographic views or when only relative camera motion is available without scale/intrinsics.
